Step 2 :- Applying imputation(getting value for filling missing data) 
	
	si_age = SimpleImputer() # will give mean value

	⚙️ 1️⃣ fit()

		Learns (computes) some parameters from the training data.

		For example,

		In SimpleImputer(strategy='mean'), it calculates the mean of that column.

		In a scaler, it computes min, max, mean, std, etc.

		si_age.fit(X_train[['Age']])

	⚙️ 2️⃣ transform()

		Actually applies the learned parameters (from fit()) to the data.

		It doesn’t learn anything new — it just uses what was learned earlier.

		X_train_age = si_age.transform(X_train[['Age']])
		X_test_age = si_age.transform(X_test[['Age']])

	⚙️ 3️⃣ fit_transform()

		A shortcut for doing both fit and transform together on the same data.

		Commonly used only on training data.

		X_train_age = si_age.fit_transform(X_train[['Age']])

